<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brown v. Board of Education</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brown v. Board of Education was a unanimous ruling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Supreme Court on 17 May 1954 that state laws establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> in public schools violated the Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause. The case began in Topeka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, was heard with four similar cases and was argued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>'s chief counsel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgood-marshall">Thurgood Marshall</a>. In Chief Justice Earl Warren's words, &quot;separate educational facilities are inherently unequal&quot;; the decision partially overruled Plessy v. Ferguson of 1896. A second decision in 1955 only ordered states to desegregate &quot;with all deliberate speed&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education.jpg" alt="Brown v. Board of Education" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Press International telephoto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warren_Court_1953.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
